WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.817
Every team has someone who knows the project inside out.

2
00:00:03.817 --> 00:00:12.855
When that person leaves, a lot of what they know leaves with them: why things were built a certain way, who owns what, and what is quietly broken.

3
00:00:12.855 --> 00:00:16.570
KnowBridge keeps that knowledge, and hands it over properly.

4
00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:19.863
It works in six simple steps.

5
00:00:19.863 --> 00:00:23.184
One, connect the tools your team already uses.

6
00:00:23.184 --> 00:00:28.525
Two, KnowBridge reads everything, and hides passwords and private details.

7
00:00:28.525 --> 00:00:35.166
Three, AI agents turn it into clear facts, called claims, each linked to where it came from.

8
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:38.053
Four, a person checks and approves them.

9
00:00:38.053 --> 00:00:42.023
Five, the person leaving answers a few short questions.

10
00:00:42.023 --> 00:00:47.220
And six, the new owner gets a handover pack, and a chat to ask anything.

11
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.469
Before we start, four words you will hear a lot.

12
00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:58.140
A source is a place where your team works, like a WhatsApp group, a GitHub repository, or a Trello board.

13
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:06.144
Evidence is one single item from a source, like one chat message, saved exactly as it was written, with who wrote it and when.

14
00:01:06.144 --> 00:01:13.387
A claim is one short, clear fact that the AI pulls out of the evidence, like: ticket payments go through Razorpay.

15
00:01:13.387 --> 00:01:18.151
Every claim keeps the exact words it came from, so you can always check it.

16
00:01:18.151 --> 00:01:23.170
And the claim ledger is the list of all the claims for a project, in one place.

17
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.704
Now let's use it for real, from the very start.

18
00:01:27.704 --> 00:01:32.457
Our example is Project Nimbus, the web app for a college tech fest.

19
00:01:32.457 --> 00:01:37.210
Aarav, the lead developer, is graduating, and Meera will take over.

20
00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.260
We sign in as Kabir, the club's tech admin.

21
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.435
First, we create a project.

22
00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:49.360
Click New project, type a name and a short description, and press Create project.

23
00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.224
Next, we add the people.

24
00:01:52.224 --> 00:01:53.888
Each person gets a role.

25
00:01:53.888 --> 00:01:57.078
Aarav is leaving, so he gets the Leaving role.

26
00:01:57.078 --> 00:01:58.673
Meera gets Taking over.

27
00:01:58.673 --> 00:02:04.220
And Priya is the approver, the person who checks everything before it is shared.

28
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.340
Roles decide what each person can see and do.

29
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:10.774
Now we connect the team's tools.

30
00:02:10.774 --> 00:02:12.311
These are the sources.

31
00:02:12.311 --> 00:02:15.104
Click Connect a source, and pick GitHub.

32
00:02:15.104 --> 00:02:20.411
Type the repository name, and add a token only if the repository is private.

33
00:02:20.411 --> 00:02:24.740
For this demo, we upload a snapshot of the repository instead.

34
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.094
Task boards work the same way.

35
00:02:28.094 --> 00:02:36.999
If your team uses Jira, choose Connect Jira Cloud, then enter your Jira site, your email, an API token, and the project key.

36
00:02:36.999 --> 00:02:39.369
It connects live, and only reads.

37
00:02:39.369 --> 00:02:44.180
Our team uses Trello, so we upload the board's export file instead.

38
00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.060
We add the meeting notes, and the Discord and WhatsApp chats, in the same way.

39
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.620
We have sped this part up.

40
00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:56.207
KnowBridge now reads each source in the background.

41
00:02:56.207 --> 00:03:00.724
Every message, commit, card and paragraph becomes one evidence item.

42
00:03:00.724 --> 00:03:10.753
On the way in, a guard called Sentinel hides secrets, like passwords and phone numbers, and labels anything sensitive, so only the right people see it.

43
00:03:10.753 --> 00:03:12.479
Here is the Evidence page.

44
00:03:12.479 --> 00:03:17.660
Look: a password someone shared in the WhatsApp group has already been hidden.

45
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.230
Now the clever part.

46
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:25.096
When we click Build claims, a team of AI agents reads all the evidence.

47
00:03:25.096 --> 00:03:26.398
The Scout sorts it.

48
00:03:26.398 --> 00:03:30.852
Specialist agents find decisions, owners, dependencies and risks.

49
00:03:30.852 --> 00:03:34.415
The Timeline Builder notices when something changed.

50
00:03:34.415 --> 00:03:39.760
And the Skeptic checks every claim against its evidence, and gives it a grade.

51
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.484
Here they are, working on our project.

52
00:03:43.484 --> 00:03:44.879
We have sped this up.

53
00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:47.270
Each step shows what that agent did.

54
00:03:49.754 --> 00:03:53.730
And this is the result: the claim ledger for Project Nimbus.

55
00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.254
Each claim has a grade.

56
00:03:55.254 --> 00:03:58.038
Verified means two separate sources agree.

57
00:03:58.038 --> 00:04:00.755
Supported means one clear source says it.

58
00:04:00.755 --> 00:04:03.737
Inferred means the evidence only hints at it.

59
00:04:03.737 --> 00:04:08.045
And Conflicting means sources disagree, so someone has to decide.

60
00:04:08.045 --> 00:04:11.425
Open any claim to see the exact words it came from.

61
00:04:11.425 --> 00:04:15.004
You can also search the ledger with a normal question.

62
00:04:16.204 --> 00:04:19.987
Before anyone leaves, it helps to know where the danger is.

63
00:04:19.987 --> 00:04:28.515
The Risk Radar looks at three things for every part of the project: who wrote the code, who owns the tickets, and who talks about it.

64
00:04:28.515 --> 00:04:35.120
If one person holds almost all the knowledge about an area, and nobody else does, that area is at risk.

65
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.749
The AI only groups the claims into areas.

66
00:04:37.749 --> 00:04:42.174
The scores are worked out by plain code, so they are always the same.

67
00:04:43.374 --> 00:04:45.949
Here is the Risk Radar for our project.

68
00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:50.440
At the top, it shows what would happen if each person left tomorrow.

69
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.714
Below is the knowledge map: every area of the project, and how much each person knows about it.

70
00:04:56.714 --> 00:04:58.696
The riskiest areas come first.

71
00:04:58.696 --> 00:05:08.074
And with Capture knowledge, the one person who knows an area answers a few short questions, so that knowledge is saved before anyone needs it.

72
00:05:09.274 --> 00:05:13.509
Now we start the handover, which means moving Aarav's work to Meera.

73
00:05:13.509 --> 00:05:18.989
Pick who is leaving, who is taking over, and who approves, and add the last working day.

74
00:05:18.989 --> 00:05:21.294
Then click Prepare the Gap Interview.

75
00:05:22.494 --> 00:05:33.942
The Gap Interview is a short list of questions for the person leaving, about things the evidence does not answer: missing owners, unclear decisions, or facts that disagree.

76
00:05:33.942 --> 00:05:35.806
The AI writes the questions.

77
00:05:35.806 --> 00:05:37.204
This part is sped up.

78
00:05:38.405 --> 00:05:39.969
Now we sign in as Aarav.

79
00:05:39.969 --> 00:05:43.684
He sees his questions, and answers them in his own words.

80
00:05:43.684 --> 00:05:48.442
If he doesn't know, he can skip it, or pass the question to someone else.

81
00:05:48.442 --> 00:05:53.265
His answers go straight back into the claim ledger, and update the grades.

82
00:05:54.465 --> 00:05:56.174
Next is Priya, the approver.

83
00:05:56.174 --> 00:05:58.616
Nothing is shared until she approves it.

84
00:05:58.616 --> 00:06:02.645
She approves the claims that are right, and clicks Write the pack.

85
00:06:04.637 --> 00:06:10.180
The handover pack is a guide written for the new owner, built only from approved claims.

86
00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.707
When Priya is happy with it, she publishes it for Meera.

87
00:06:14.909 --> 00:06:18.703
Finally, Meera signs in, and her handover pack is ready.

88
00:06:18.703 --> 00:06:21.006
It starts with what to read first.

89
00:06:21.006 --> 00:06:29.135
Then what she is taking over, the key decisions and why they were made, who owns what, the risks, and a thirty day plan.

90
00:06:29.135 --> 00:06:31.709
Every line links back to its evidence.

91
00:06:32.909 --> 00:06:36.224
Projects keep changing after a handover is written.

92
00:06:36.224 --> 00:06:40.059
So KnowBridge reads the team's tools again every few hours.

93
00:06:40.059 --> 00:06:46.819
When something a claim quotes is edited or deleted, it first checks if the quoted words are still there.

94
00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:50.069
If they are, nothing changes, and no AI is needed.

95
00:06:50.069 --> 00:06:53.904
If the words changed, the Skeptic reads the new text again.

96
00:06:53.904 --> 00:07:00.339
If the claim no longer holds, it goes back to the approver, and the new owner is told what changed.

97
00:07:01.581 --> 00:07:02.463
Let's try it.

98
00:07:02.463 --> 00:07:07.212
Imagine a teammate edits an old chat message, and reverses a decision.

99
00:07:07.212 --> 00:07:10.332
In demo mode, we can make that change by hand.

100
00:07:10.332 --> 00:07:15.691
We open the message one of the approved claims came from, and change its words.

101
00:07:16.891 --> 00:07:20.523
A few seconds later, the Skeptic has read the new text.

102
00:07:20.523 --> 00:07:24.881
The claim is now marked as contradicted, and it is back in review.

103
00:07:24.881 --> 00:07:29.503
Opening it shows exactly what changed, the old words and the new ones.

104
00:07:29.503 --> 00:07:35.181
And in Meera's handover pack, that line now says: changed since this pack was written.

105
00:07:36.381 --> 00:07:39.763
Meera can also just ask questions in the project chat.

106
00:07:39.763 --> 00:07:41.079
Here is how it works.

107
00:07:41.079 --> 00:07:50.537
It turns her question into numbers that capture its meaning, finds the closest pieces from all the evidence and claims, and gives only those to the AI.

108
00:07:50.537 --> 00:07:53.731
The AI must answer from them, and show its sources.

109
00:07:54.931 --> 00:07:57.245
Let's ask why the builds are failing.

110
00:07:57.245 --> 00:08:01.123
The answer comes with numbered sources she can click to check.

111
00:08:01.123 --> 00:08:04.751
And she can ask a follow up question, like who can fix it.

112
00:08:05.951 --> 00:08:10.042
Developers spend their day in a code editor, not on a website.

113
00:08:10.042 --> 00:08:12.418
So KnowBridge also comes to VS Code.

114
00:08:12.418 --> 00:08:19.413
Open any file, and a panel shows who changed it, who created it, and the decisions and risks linked to it.

115
00:08:19.413 --> 00:08:23.372
You can ask the project chat about the file you are reading.

116
00:08:23.372 --> 00:08:29.971
And AI coding assistants, like Copilot or Claude, can read the same knowledge through an MCP server.

117
00:08:31.171 --> 00:08:35.817
To connect your editor, open the Editor tab, and make an access token.

118
00:08:35.817 --> 00:08:41.525
It can only read, it opens only this one project, and it can be cancelled at any time.

119
00:08:41.525 --> 00:08:47.631
Then install the extension, paste the token, and the panel fills in for every file you open.

120
00:08:48.832 --> 00:08:51.484
Now let's see it for real, in VS Code.

121
00:08:51.484 --> 00:08:53.647
Meera opens the project's code.

122
00:08:53.647 --> 00:08:59.997
She installs the KnowBridge extension from the file she downloaded, with Install from VSIX.

123
00:08:59.997 --> 00:09:01.602
It takes a few seconds.

124
00:09:02.802 --> 00:09:05.095
Then she runs KnowBridge: Connect.

125
00:09:05.095 --> 00:09:09.074
She types the website address, and pastes her access token.

126
00:09:09.074 --> 00:09:12.176
VS Code keeps the token in its secure storage.

127
00:09:12.176 --> 00:09:16.222
KnowBridge is now connected to the Nimbus project, as Meera.

128
00:09:17.422 --> 00:09:19.763
She opens a file, webhooks dot ts.

129
00:09:19.763 --> 00:09:26.303
The KnowBridge panel shows who changed it and who created it, straight from the GitHub history.

130
00:09:26.303 --> 00:09:34.152
Below are the owners, decisions and risks linked to this file, each with its grade, and a reason why it is linked.

131
00:09:35.356 --> 00:09:40.345
Now she asks about the file she is reading: who should I talk to about this file?

132
00:09:40.345 --> 00:09:45.827
The answer comes only from the project's own record, with numbered sources she can check.

133
00:09:45.827 --> 00:09:46.566
No guessing.

134
00:09:47.766 --> 00:09:54.127
KnowBridge also works with AI coding assistants, through MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

135
00:09:54.127 --> 00:10:03.776
In the terminal, one command adds the KnowBridge MCP server to Claude Code: the server file, the website address, and the access token.

136
00:10:04.976 --> 00:10:10.939
Now we start Claude Code, and ask in plain words: who should I talk to about the webhooks file?

137
00:10:10.939 --> 00:10:20.670
Claude calls KnowBridge's tools, reads the project's checked facts, and answers with who owns it, the evidence behind it, and anything that doesn't add up.

138
00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:23.746
The same knowledge, wherever the developer works.

139
00:10:24.931 --> 00:10:26.745
The chat is protected too.

140
00:10:26.745 --> 00:10:30.861
Layers of guardrails check every question and every answer.

141
00:10:30.861 --> 00:10:36.720
Hidden instructions inside messages are removed, and links or secrets never get out.

142
00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:42.161
We tested it with eighty five attacks, in seven languages, and nothing leaked.

143
00:10:43.361 --> 00:10:44.283
Let's try one.

144
00:10:44.283 --> 00:10:46.326
We tell it to ignore its rules.

145
00:10:46.326 --> 00:10:53.901
It refuses straight away, and the attempt is saved in the audit log, where every action in the project is recorded.

146
00:10:55.101 --> 00:10:56.254
That's KnowBridge.

147
00:10:56.254 --> 00:11:07.207
Connect your tools, let the agents find the facts, see where knowledge is at risk, fill the gaps with a short interview, keep it up to date, and hand over with confidence.

148
00:11:07.207 --> 00:11:09.641
Nothing gets lost when people move on.
